%[text] %[text:anchor:T_BB73E10C] # Algorithms
%[text] [⇦ Main Menu](file:../MainMenu.m)
%[text] The formal name for a clearly defined process that could be followed by a computer is an algorithm. You already know several algorithms from mathematics, and you may have encountered them elsewhere in your life as well for processes like searching for a word in a paper dictionary or sorting a hand of cards into a standard order. You may be less familiar with expressing algorithms themselves clearly and concisely.
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![A flowchart presentation of the sieve of Eratosthenes, as presented in section "Coding a Simple Math Problem"](text:image:500b)
%[text:tableOfContents]{"heading":"**Table of Contents**"}
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_85A88EA9] ## Communicating Algorithms
%[text] There are many ways to express an algorithm, but the most common are to write out the procedure in standard sentences or in pseudocode. Pseudocode is a term that means a small collection of formal words and symbols that can be combined with formatting and small amounts of text to describe an algorithm that could be implemented in any programming language by a programmer who is familiar with the language.
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_A89F2D0A] ### Common features of pseudocode
%[text] 1. A symbol that denotes that the rest of the line is a comment. Coming from a MATLAB environment, this script will use `%` as the comment character.
%[text] 2. `a` $\\leftarrow${"altText":"is assigned"} `b` means "a is assigned the value of b"
%[text] 3. Standard mathematical symbols such as +, \*, \< as well as mathematical symbols from discrete mathematics such as \\ ("set difference"), ~ ("logical not"), & ("logical and"), and == ("logical equals").
%[text] 4. Sequence identification `startPoint to endPoint` means the set of value \
%[text]{"align":"center"} `{StartPoint, StartPoint + 1, StartPoint + 2, ..., EndPoint - 2, EndPoint -1, EndPoint`}`.` 
%[text]             Similarly, if the step size between values isn't 1, that can be indicated by 
%[text]{"align":"center"} `StartPoint by StepSize to EndPoint` 
%[text]             to mean the list of values 
%[text]{"align":"center"} `{StartPoint, StartPoint + StepSize, StartPoint + 2*StepSize, ... , StartPoint+m*StepSize}` 
%[text]             where (`EndPoint-StartPoint)/(m+1) < StepSize, but (EndPointSstartPoint)/m` $\\geq${"altText":"greater than or equal to"} S`tepSize.`
%[text]           5\. Standard control logic: `while, for, if/else.` 
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_28870F4B] ### Example: Expressing the Simple Square Chaos Game as an Algorithm
%[text] `StartingPoint` $\\leftarrow${"altText":"is assigned"} `random point`
%[text] `NumPts` $\\leftarrow${"altText":"is assigned"} `number of iterations`
%[text] `for k = 1 to NumPts`
%[text]  `NewCorner` $\\leftarrow${"altText":"is assigned"} `randomly selected corner coordinates`
%[text]  `NextPoint` $\\leftarrow${"altText":"is assigned"} `StartingPoint + (NewCorner-StartingPoint)/2`
%[text]  `Graph NextPoint`
%[text]  `StartingPoint` $\\leftarrow${"altText":"is assigned"} N`extPoint`
%[text] 
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_80432ACB] ## Coding a Simple Math Problem
%[text] What are all the primes less than or equal to 100? This is a computationally straight-forward problem with several common algorithmic solutions. A positive integer $n${"altText":"n"} is prime if its only factors are $1${"altText":"1"} and $n${"altText":"n"}. 
%[text] **Algorithm 1:** Iteratively build the list of all primes less than or equal to $n${"altText":"n"} up to `MaximumValue`
%[text]  `% Recognize that 2 is the smallest prime.` 
%[text]  `% When n=2, the only prime less than or equal to n is` $2${"altText":"2"}`.`
%[text]  `PrimeList` $\\leftarrow${"altText":"is assigned"} `2`
%[text]  `for n in 3 to MaximumValue`
%[text]  `for p in PrimeList`
%[text]  `if n` [`mod`](https://www.mathworks.com/help/matlab/ref/double.mod.html) `p == 0`
%[text]  `n is composite and can be ignored`
%[text]  `else if no primes in PrimeList are factors of n`
%[text]  `append n to PrimeList`
PrimeList = 2;
for n = 3:100
    NotPrimeFlag = 0;
    for p = PrimeList
        if mod(n,p) == 0
            NotPrimeFlag = 1;
        elseif p == PrimeList(end) && NotPrimeFlag == 0
            PrimeList = [PrimeList n];
        end
    end
end
PrimeList
%%
%[text] **Algorithm 2:** Assume all numbers are possible up to `maximumValue`, then iteratively eliminate multiples of smaller values.
%[text]  `primeList` $\\leftarrow${"altText":"is assigned"} `2 to maximumValue`
%[text]  `p` $\\leftarrow${"altText":"is assigned"} `2`
%[text]  `index` $\\leftarrow${"altText":"is assigned"} `1`
%[text]  `while p < sqrt(maximumValue)`
%[text]  `primeList` $\\leftarrow${"altText":"is assigned"} `primeList \ (2*p by p to maximumValue)`
%[text]  `index` $\\leftarrow${"altText":"is assigned"} `index + 1`
%[text]  `p` $\\leftarrow${"altText":"is assigned"} `primeList(index)`
%[text] ![](text:image:9cd7) **Exercise 1**.
